#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] second couple in a family are parents and children.

In laying down their duties, the apostle begins with children. His direction and urging to them is laid down in the first three verses of the sixth chapter. In these verses:

+ He declares their #emph[duty.]
+ He adds #emph[reasons] to enforce it.

In laying down their duty he notes three points.

+ #emph[In what] it consists #emph[(obey, honor).]
+ #emph[To whom] it is to be performed #emph[(your#super[+] parents).]
+ #emph[In what manner] it is to be done #emph[(in the Lord).]

The reasons the apostle uses are four.

+ The #emph[place] of parents #emph[(in the Lord).]
+ The #emph[justice] of the thing #emph[(this is right).]
+ The #emph[charge] of God #emph[(honor your father,] etc.)
+ The #emph[reward] promised #emph[(that it may go well,] etc.)

Under this word #emph[(obey),] which the apostle uses, and that word #emph[(honor),] which the law uses, are included all the duties that are enjoined on children anywhere throughout the whole of Scripture.

We will therefore set them down in some order, and handle them separately, one after another.

+ The #emph[fountain] of children’s duties must be searched out.
+ The #emph[streams] that flow from it must be observed.

The #emph[fountain] is an inward disposition of the heart, compounded of #emph[love] and #emph[fear.]

The #emph[streams] issuing from it extend to parents both while they are #emph[living] and also when they are #emph[dead.]

The duties children are to perform to their parents while they live concern their

- #emph[Authority.]
- #emph[Necessity.]

The #emph[authority] of parents requires of children

- #emph[Reverence.]
- #emph[Obedience.]

Their #emph[necessity] requires #emph[recompense.]

The duties children owe to their deceased parents concern their

- #emph[Body.]
- #emph[Reputation.]

Their #emph[body] must be #emph[buried] with decency.

Their #emph[reputation] must be #emph[maintained] with honor.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of children’s love for their parents.]

I make the fountain of children’s duties a mixed and compound disposition, because of the #emph[authority and affection] that are mixed together in parents. The #emph[authority] of parents requires #emph[fear] in children, and their #emph[affection, love.] So entire and so ardent is parents’ #emph[affection] toward their children that it would make children too bold and insolent if there were no #emph[authority] mixed with it to produce #emph[fear.] And so supreme and absolute is their #emph[authority] over them that it would make children dread their parents like slaves, if a fatherly #emph[affection] were not tempered with it to breed #emph[love.] But both of these joined together make a very good mixture: #emph[love,] like sugar, sweetens #emph[fear,] and #emph[fear,] like salt, seasons #emph[love.] And so, to join them both together, it is a #emph[loving fear,] or a #emph[fearing love,] that is the ground of children’s duties.

Where Christ forbids an excessive love in children for #footnote[Matt 10:37.] their parents, he implies that parents are a fit object for children to love (so long as their love is well moderated). Indeed, he implies that love for their parents is a feeling grafted into children even by nature. Joseph is commended to children as a worthy pattern in loving his father, and that from his youth until his father’s death. As evidence of this, in his younger years he brought his father “a bad report” about his #footnote[Gen 37:2.] brothers, by which he incurred their envy and hatred; he would never have done this if he had not loved his father. And having been long absent from his father, when by God’s providence an occasion was offered for him to meet his brothers, one of his first questions to them was about their father; #footnote[Gen 43:7.] and hearing that he was alive, he did not think it enough to send him food for his need, but must also see his face #footnote[Gen 45:9.] and have him live with him. And while his father was on the way, he went out to meet him, and at first sight fell on his neck #footnote[Gen 46:29.] and wept a good while (a token of great affection).

The love that parents naturally bear their children #footnote[Reasons. 1. Justice.] ought in justice to breed in children a love for their parents. For love deserves love, and those who cannot love in return are most unworthy to be loved. The love of parents, above all others, must be answered with love on the children’s part to the utmost of their power, because it is free, great and constant.

Besides, love in children toward their parents is a necessity, #footnote[2\. Necessity.] for without it their subjection (which of all others ought to be most free) would turn into slavish servitude.

Children ought to labor after this all the more, because by nature they are nowhere near as prone to love their parents as their parents are to love them. Love is weighty, and like weighty things it descends. Children therefore, out of conscience of their duty, must labor to make up this defect, and help nature by grace. I do not deny that there is naturally in children a greater love for their parents than for others; yet compared with the heat of their parents’ love for them, their love for their parents is cold. Therefore, just as the heat of the sun, shining much and long on a stone wall, draws a reflection of heat from that wall, so the hot beams of parents’ love, cast on children with fervency and constancy, ought to provoke and stir up children to send back a reflection of love on their parents.

Two extremes are contrary to this love.

One is #emph[lack of natural affection,] a vice most odious #footnote[ἀστοργία. Rom 1:31. 2 Tim 3:3.] and abominable in all, but most of all in children. The apostle counts this among the most heinous vices there are.

The other is hatred and spite toward parents, a vice more than monstrous and against nature. From it come the mocking and cursing of parents, of which we will hear later.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of a child’s fear of his parent.]

To the duty of love named above must be added #emph[fear,] which is a child’s #emph[awe-filled respect for his parent.]

This #emph[awe-filled respect] arises from an honorable esteem that a child has of his parent in his judgment and opinion, as he is his parent. From it proceeds, on the one side, a desire and endeavor to please the parent in all things, and, on the other side, a reluctance to offend him.

In this respect the fear of a #emph[child] is set against the fear of a #emph[slave.] For a child’s fear, being mixed with love, looks to the #emph[offense] a parent may take; but a slave’s fear, which is ordinarily mixed with hatred, looks to nothing but the #emph[punishment] his master may inflict on him. The fear named above belongs so distinctively to children that the awe-filled respect the saints bear to God is called a #footnote[See Treatise 1, §. 4.] #emph[filial] or #emph[childlike fear.]

This #emph[fear] in a child is a special branch of that #emph[honor]#footnote[Exod 20:12.] which the law requires of children toward their parents, and it is in #footnote[Lev 19:3.] express terms enjoined on children by the law. The words God uses of Miriam#footnote[Num 12:14.] (“If her father had but spit in her face, would she not have been ashamed for seven days?”) show that there ought to be such a fear of the parent in a child’s heart as would work shame in it when the parent is offended.

We have a worthy pattern of this in Jacob,#footnote[Gen 27:12.] who was loath to gain the blessing by offending his father.

This #emph[fear] keeps #emph[love] within bounds, and restrains a child #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. Fear orders love.] from too much sauciness and impudence.

And it is a cause of a child’s reverent and dutiful conduct #footnote[2\. It guides conduct well.] toward his parent. For as the heart is disposed, so the conduct will be ordered.

Contrary to this is that #emph[slight] esteem of parents, or (what is more abominable) #footnote[Contrary, slight and base esteem of parents.] that #emph[base] and #emph[vile] esteem of them, which is in the hearts of many children, especially if their parents are poor, of low degree, unlearned, ignorant, or subject to any weaknesses. Ham#footnote[Gen 9:22.] must have had too slight an esteem of his father, if not a base one, when he mocked him. A true filial fear would have restrained him from that extreme.

Therefore, to breed and cherish this fear, and to prevent #footnote[How to breed and cherish a filial fear.] or remedy the contrary extreme, let children inform themselves well of their parents’ place and authority: how they are in God’s place, and a means under God of their children’s being. Children have received their very substance from the substance of their parents. In this respect, though parents should seem contemptible to others, yet they must not seem so to their children.

So much for a child’s inward disposition toward his parent. It must be shown by his outward conduct, and that in two things, #emph[reverence] and #emph[obedience,] both of which concern a parent’s authority.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of a child’s] reverence #emph[in refraining from speech before his parent, and in listening to his parent.]

The outward #emph[reverence] that children owe to their parents consists partly in their #emph[speech,] partly in their conduct.

Their speech both #emph[to] and #emph[about] their parents must savor of reverence: to their parents in their presence, about their parents in their absence.

In their presence, by #emph[refraining from] speech and by #emph[framing] it #emph[well.]

For refraining from speech two virtues are needed:

- #emph[Silence.]
- #emph[Patience.]

Silence, in #emph[refraining] from speaking and in #emph[breaking off] speech. #footnote[Children ought to refrain from speaking in their parents’ presence]

Patience, in listening to their parents.

The two branches of silence, refraining from speaking (especially when parents are speaking, or until parents give their children leave to speak), and breaking off speech when parents come into the place where children are speaking, are tokens of great reverence. In this way children testify that someone is present whom they greatly respect and honor. Job sets out in this way the respect that princes and others bore him in his prosperity: “The princes” (he says) “refrained #footnote[Job 29:9, 10.] from speaking and covered their mouths with their hands. The voices of the nobles were hushed,” etc. That is, while he was present, or while he spoke. The same may be said of children’s patience in enduring #footnote[Children must patiently listen to their parents’ speech. #emph[v.] 21.] their parents’ speech, which Job also notes in these words: “Men listened to me with expectation, waiting silently.” Though parents may seem long and tedious in their speech, yet children must endure it.

And it is very needful that patience be added to silence, because many parents, out of tender love for their children and earnest desire for their good, think they can never say enough in instructing and admonishing them. The many exhortations #footnote[Gen 49:2. Prov 1:8; 4:1; 7:1.] given to children in Scripture to #emph[hear, listen, give ear, pay attention, mark] and #emph[observe] the words of their parents imply the #emph[silence] and #emph[patience] named above. For those who #footnote[Jas 1:19.] ought to be “quick to listen” must be “slow to speak.” I do not deny that much more is intended by those phrases, namely #emph[obedience;] yet #emph[these] must also be presupposed. For he who will not listen patiently and in silence to his parents while they speak will much less obey what they say.

Contrary to silence is sauciness (as we say) and too much #footnote[Contrary, 1. boldness of speech.] boldness in children, when, without due respect for their parents’ presence, they will be chattering about this thing or that, so much so that if strangers came into the room where such children are, they would not think the children’s parents were present; or if they knew it, they might well think that such children bear little respect for their parents.

Contrary to patience in hearing is fretting and grumbling #footnote[2\. Impatient listening to parents.] against parents (at least if their speech is at all long), and flouncing off or slinking away before they have finished. These faults are all the greater if children, by their talkativeness or impatience, hinder or interrupt their parents’ speech when they are giving any admonition or instruction. For in this way they show both too slight a respect for their parents and too little regard for the means of their own good.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of a child’s reverent framing of his speech to his parent.]

A child’s reverence in framing his speech well to his parents #footnote[A child’s reverence in speech is shown, 1. By reverent titles.] may be shown in many ways, as

1\. By giving them reverent and honorable titles. No title can be more honorable than the one that most properly belongs to them and is most usual: #emph[father] for the one parent, and #emph[mother] for the other. God takes the title #emph[father]#footnote[Jer 31:9. Gal 4:6.] to himself, as a title of great dignity.

#emph[Objection.] This title belongs so exclusively to God that #footnote[Matt 23:9.] #emph[we are to call no one on earth Father.]

#emph[Answer.] This is not to be taken absolutely of the title itself, but of the mind of the one who gives that title or covets it.

If it is coveted or given so as to obscure God’s #emph[Fatherhood,] or to make a man #emph[a father] of himself, without dependence on God or reference to him, who is properly the Father of all, it is an impious and sacrilegious title. But otherwise it is lawful and warrantable.

In Scripture the title #emph[father] is given to every degree of dignity among men: to #emph[kings,]#footnote[1 Sam 24:12.] #emph[captains,]#footnote[2 Kgs 5:13.] and other #emph[chief governors;] to #emph[priests,]#footnote[Judg 18:19.] #emph[prophets,]#footnote[2 Kgs 6:21.] #emph[apostles,]#footnote[1 Cor 4:15.] and other #emph[ministers.]#footnote[1 Tim 5:1.] In the fifth commandment all superiors are included under it; therefore #emph[father] is a title of great honor. And by the rule of relation, #emph[mother] is a title of equally great honor for the female sex. Religious and dutiful children have always been accustomed to give those titles to their parents. “My father,” says Isaac#footnote[Gen 22:7.] to Abraham, and Jacob#footnote[Gen 27:18.] to Isaac. “My mother,”#footnote[1 Kgs 2:20.] says Solomon to Bathsheba. I also find the title #emph[sir]#footnote[Matt 21:30.] or #emph[lord] used: a title #footnote[κύριε. Gen 31:35. אדני] of honor.

2\. By using #emph[few words] before their parents, and those few #footnote[2\. By few words.] not without just occasion: when they are first spoken to by their parents, or have leave from them, or are making some needful matter known to them; at least not so against their parents’ liking that their parents would be offended by it. And if they observe that their parents are unwilling to hear them speak any more of such and such a matter, then they ought to lay their hands on their mouths, as Isaac (Gen 22:7) and Jacob (Gen 27:12) did. This is a token of great respect.

3\. By meek and humble speech. Such was the speech of #footnote[3\. By humble speech.] Jonathan,#footnote[1 Sam 19:4.] Saul’s natural son, and of David,#footnote[1 Sam 24:10.] his son-in-law, with which Saul was much pleased and his wrath pacified.

4\. By watching for a fit opportunity: as when parents are not #footnote[4\. By well-timed speech.] seriously busy, or in company, or angry. When Saul#footnote[1 Sam 19:6.] was not angry, how well he accepted Jonathan’s defense of David! But #footnote[1 Sam 20:30.] in his anger, how badly he took it! This wise watching for a fit opportunity shows great reverence.

5\. By a prompt, ready, willing and pleasing answer when they are spoken to by #footnote[5\. By ready answers.] their parents. Eli was like a father to Samuel; therefore when Samuel#footnote[1 Sam 3:4, 6.] thought that Eli called him, he answered at once and readily, “Here I am.” And when Eli pressed to know what the Lord had said to him, “Samuel told him everything and did not hide a thing from him.”#footnote[1 Sam 3:18.] #footnote[Matt 21:30.] The younger son (noted in the parable) showed a son’s reverence in giving his father a willing and ready answer, though he failed in his obedience by not doing what he promised.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of the vices in children contrary to the aforesaid reverence in speech.]

Contrary to those branches of reverence in speech are these: #footnote[Contrary, 1. Scorning to acknowledge a parent.]

1\. #emph[Pride:] when children scorn to give their parents the title of #emph[father] or #emph[mother.] This is the attitude of many who have gained more wealth or honor than their parents ever had. In public especially such children most refuse to give those titles. Solomon was not of that mind. Though he was a great king, sitting on his throne in the sight and hearing of all his people who were around him, he called Bathsheba #emph[mother.] #footnote[1 Kgs 2:20.]

If children had the regard for their parents’ honor that they should, then, being themselves in places of honor and dignity, they would all the more openly call their parents #emph[father] and #emph[mother,] so that their parents might be known to be the father and mother of so eminent a person.

2\. #emph[Talkativeness,] and too much insistence, or rather impudence #footnote[2\. Too much insistence.] in speech: when children, in dealing with their parents, can never have done (as we say), but must press matters to the very utmost. Many parents are often greatly provoked by this. It does not matter that the child is in the right, especially in a matter of no great consequence. For reverence’s sake the child must hold back, at least for a time. And if the matter in dispute is weighty, as in points of religion, the child must either take some other opportunity of better informing his parent, or else get some other wise friend to do it.

3\. #emph[Defiance,] when children answer their parents as if they #footnote[3\. Defiant answers. #emph[Ne filius cum patre quoquo modo expostulet, cum lex interdixit, tum anteleges ipsa natura. Greg. Naz. in senten.] \[Let a son not dispute with his father in any way: the law has forbidden it, and, before the laws, nature itself.\] Matt 21:29. Luke 15:29.] were their equals, giving word for word. It is as unbecoming for children to #emph[answer back] as for servants (whom the apostle has expressly forbidden to do it, Titus 2:9). Both law and nature forbid children to be provoked to this by anything their parents say or do. How great, then, is the fault of those who give scornful and defiant words to their parents when they are not provoked at all, as the #emph[elder son] noted in the parable did, and the #emph[elder brother] of the prodigal son!

4\. #emph[Indiscretion,] when children, in speaking to their parents, have no regard for their parents’ time, business or mood, and #footnote[4\. Ill-timed speech. Eph 6:4.] so greatly provoke them. It is laid down as a caution to parents that they must not provoke their children to wrath. How much more must children observe that caution?

5\. #emph[Stubbornness,] when children pout, scowl, sulk and give #footnote[5\. Sullen silence.] their parents no answer at all. This is too common a fault in children, and many parents are much offended and grieved by it. We heard #footnote[§. 4.] before of a childlike silence that was very commendable and a token of great reverence; but this is worthy of much blame, a token of great undutifulness, and is to be avoided as carefully as that is to be practiced.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of children’s reverent speech about their parents.]

The reverent respect that children bear their parents ought to be so true and entire that their speech, not only #emph[to them] before their faces but also #emph[about them] behind their backs, must be so framed in both matter and manner, whenever they have occasion to talk about their parents, that all who hear them may note that they bear a reverent respect for their parents. #footnote[Direction for children on how to speak about their parents.]

As a general direction for performing this duty better, let children say nothing about their parents that they would be unwilling to have come to their parents’ ears. More particularly, let them speak of the things that most tend to their parents’ praise, so that (as Christ said of his Father#footnote[John 8:49.]) they may honor their parents. Let other things be buried in silence, as far as they can. And if others speak of matters disgraceful to their parents, let them put the better construction on things doubtful, and, as far as they may, lessen things evident, and sharply rebuke those who slander their parents. This is the #emph[blessing] that children owe to their parents. For neglecting it the wise man reproaches children, saying, “There is a generation that … does not bless its #footnote[Prov 30:11.] mother.”

Contrary to that kind of blessing is exposing parents’ #footnote[Children’s irreverent speech about their parents.] infirmities, noted in cursed Ham;#footnote[Gen 9:22.] and spreading untruths about them, noted in impious Absalom;#footnote[2 Sam 15:3.] and mocking and cursing them, #footnote[Prov 30:11.] which is expressly condemned. The reward of this by #footnote[Lev 20:9.] God’s law is death; indeed, a shameful and ignominious death, for “the ravens of the valley will pluck out his eye, and young vultures will devour it.”#footnote[Prov 30:17.] #footnote[Gen 40:19.] This phrase sets out the end of a notorious criminal who is hanged.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of a child’s reverent conduct toward his parent.]

As their speech, so the conduct of children toward their parents must be seasoned with reverence, because:

+ #emph[This] is a fruit and proof of filial fear as well as #emph[that.]
+ Of the two, this is the surer evidence, for actions are better signs of the disposition of the heart than words.
+ Fair words joined with contrary deeds cannot be counted anything but mere show and hypocrisy.
+ Where words and deeds contradict each other, the one will be a witness against the other, and that man’s condemnation the greater. #footnote[Children’s reverence to their parents shown, 1. by meeting them.]

Therefore let all reverence be shown in children’s behavior toward their parents, and that in these and similar instances.

1\. If a parent is coming to a child, and the child notices it, let him hurry to meet his parent. So Joseph#footnote[Gen 46:29.] did to his father, and #footnote[1 Kgs 2:19.] Solomon to his mother. These two examples are all the more to be noted because both were in eminent places: one a great governor, the other a king.

2\. Let such childlike gestures of respect be performed as befit #footnote[2\. By gestures of respect to them.] the child’s age and sex, whether in going to a parent, remaining before him, or going from him: such as uncovering the head, bending the knee, bowing the body, standing up, and the like. The two eminent persons named above, Joseph#footnote[Gen 48:12.] and Solomon,#footnote[1 Kgs 2:19.] bowed, the one to his father, the other to his mother.

3\. Let the expression and bearing of the body be so soberly #footnote[3\. By composing the expression well.] and modestly ordered in the parent’s presence as to show due respect.

4\. Let the upper place and precedence be given to parents; and #footnote[4\. By giving precedence.] if on occasion a child is above his parent, let him come below him. For that is a clear token of inferiority and subjection. What makes men strive for precedence, but that they want to be counted better than those with whom they strive? But that ought not to be the mind of children toward their parents.

#emph[Question.] What if children are wealthier or more honorable in their condition than their parents? Are they still to give them precedence?

#emph[Answer.] No honor is comparable to the dignity of fatherhood. It gives the parent a greater eminence over his child than any other honor can give the child over his parent. I grant that a child may be so advanced above his father by some office and outward dignity that other men may honor and reverence the child more, and give the upper place to him; and for order’s sake the child may and ought to take it in company. But when they are alone, the child must rather reverence the father.

5\. According to the custom of the time and place in which #footnote[5\. By asking a blessing.] they live, let children ask their parents’ blessing.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of children’s asking their parents’ blessing, whether it is lawful or not.]

Some doubt is raised about this duty, both with respect to the thing itself and also to the gesture of kneeling used in performing it. I will therefore prove both separately.

As for the thing itself, it is noted of Jacob that he brought savory food to his father so that his father might #emph[bless him;]#footnote[Gen 27:19.] and of Joseph, that he went to his father and took his two sons with him, #footnote[Gen 48:1. #emph[etc.]] so that his father might #emph[bless] both him and them. For this end the twelve sons of Jacob gathered to their father. #footnote[Gen 49:1. #emph[etc.] How the blessings which the patriarchs gave their children are patterns to others.]

#emph[Objection.] These were extraordinary examples. The patriarchs were endowed with the spirit of prophecy, by which they revealed to their children what their condition would be in the times to come; and their children came to them to know this.

1\. #emph[Answer.] Their blessings were more than predictions of things to come. They were confirmations and assurances to the #footnote[See Treatise 6, §. 58.] children that God would indeed perform the blessing their parents had pronounced. For they bore a double role: the role of a prophet and that of a father. As prophets they foretold things to come; as fathers they obtained the blessings pronounced,#footnote[Heb 11:20.] and an assurance of them for their children, and that by faith and prayer.

2\. #emph[Answer.] Though not every parent can, with such an extraordinary spirit, assure his children of any distinct and particular blessing, yet the faithful prayer of parents is a special and ordinary means of obtaining a blessing from God on their children; and that because of God’s promise, which extends #footnote[Gen 17:7. Acts 2:39.] not only to faithful parents but also to their descendants. Therefore, as the children of the patriarchs came to their fathers to be assured of some extraordinary blessing, so other children may go to their parents as a means of obtaining an ordinary blessing. It is noted of Elijah#footnote[Jas 5:17.] that by an extraordinary spirit in prayer he obtained extraordinary things; yet the apostle sets out that example to all Christians as a motive to stir them up to pray in faith for ordinary blessings. But to clear this point further, note the phrase #footnote[למען יארכון ימיך] used in the fifth commandment as a reason to move children to honor their parents. Word for word it is this: “that they may prolong your days,” etc. How can parents prolong their children’s days, except by begging that blessing from God? #footnote[See Treatise 6, §. 4. Prov 15:8. How wicked parents may bless their children.] The prayers of parents, then, are a great blessing to children, and children ought to seek this blessing from their parents.

#emph[Objection.] If parents are wicked, their prayer is an abomination. What blessing, then, can children look for from wicked parents?

#emph[Answer.] Though God does not hear wicked parents out of love and goodness toward themselves, yet for the good of their children he may and will hear them, and that all the more to maintain a reverent respect for parents in their children’s hearts. For asking a blessing is an acknowledgment of superiority and authority, according to the apostle’s words, “the lesser is blessed #footnote[Heb 7:7.] by the greater.”

As for the gesture of kneeling, it corresponds to the #footnote[It is lawful for children to kneel to their parents. Gen 48:12.] gesture that God’s people used of old in a similar case. Of Joseph it is said that “he bowed facedown.”

#emph[Objection.] Kneeling is a gesture belonging only to the worship of God.

#emph[Answer.] It does not belong to it so exclusively that it cannot be used in secular matters. Otherwise Christ would have reproved the young man for #emph[kneeling] before him as well as for calling him #emph[good;]#footnote[Mark 10:17.] for he took Christ to be only a man, and the worship he gave him was only social honor.

It is not the gesture alone, but the occasion of the gesture, the mind of the one who performs it, and the end for which he performs it, that make it divine or social. Cornelius fell down before Peter with the notion of some divine excellence in him, and was not allowed to do so; his manner of worship was divine. The jailer fell down before Paul and Silas in acknowledgment of some outward eminence in them, and was not reproved; his manner of worship was merely social. The same gesture may be performed to different persons with a different regard. A child may kneel to his parent and to the king, yet it does not follow that he makes his parent a king. Neither will it follow that by kneeling to his parent he makes him a God, because men kneel to God.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of the vices contrary to children’s reverent bearing toward their parents.]

Contrary to the branches of reverent bearing named above are these: #footnote[Irreverent bearing of children.]

1\. #emph[Rudeness] and #emph[bad manners,] when children do not know how to make a difference between their parents and strangers, but can #footnote[1\. Rudeness.] let their parents come to them while they stay in their place and do not stir to meet them.

2\. #emph[Disdainful haughtiness,] when they resent having to stand #footnote[2\. Haughtiness.] bareheaded for any length of time in their parents’ presence. It often happens that when parents and children are together before their betters, #emph[the parents] will show more reverence than #emph[the children.] For the father will stand bareheaded while the son sits down and puts on his hat, on the notion that his father shows more reverence than is fitting. But even if that were so, the son should stoop somewhat lower for his father’s sake.

3\. #emph[Frivolity] and #emph[boldness,] when children are overfamiliar #footnote[3\. Boldness.] with their parents, fooling about and giggling on every slight occasion. This kind of conduct cannot help but tend greatly to the disgrace and dishonor of their parents. For what can those who see it think, but that such children have been too much pampered and badly nurtured?

4\. #emph[Ambition,] when children are so ambitiously eager for place, #footnote[4\. Ambition.] especially in company, that rather than be below some whom they suppose to be at least their equals, they will be above their parents. This often happens when parents, being of a lowly mind, give place to those whom their children, being of a lofty mind, think lower than themselves. Then, rather than be under their inferiors (as they suppose), they will be above their parents: a point of great insolence. The respect of a child for his parent ought to be such that he would rather lower himself below those who are his inferiors than exalt himself above his parent. As with other men a man must, for peace’s sake, give up his right in many cases, so he must do so especially with his parent in a matter of superiority. Would not everyone who knows what honor a child owes a father condemn the ambition of a child who would so insist on place and precedence as to take them from his parent?

5\. The #emph[overscrupulous] and erroneous #emph[opinion] of those who think it #footnote[5\. Refusing to ask a blessing.] unfitting for any child to ask his parents’ blessing. Their own opinion sways them more than the continual, approved practice of God’s people in all ages. They are not unlike the man whom Solomon calls “wiser in his own eyes than seven men who answer #footnote[Prov 26:16.] discreetly.” Others, though they do not disallow this duty so generally, yet think it fitting only for young children, not considering of what years, stature and standing Joseph was when he performed it.

As for those who do not think it unlawful, yet carelessly neglect it, they little consider the benefit of a parent’s blessing. Profane Esau#footnote[Gen 27:34. Heb 12:17.] will one day rise up in judgment against them. He begged and begged, again and again, and that with a loud cry and salt tears, for a blessing from his father.

So much for children’s reverence. Their obedience follows.
